  "title": "Why Do We Dream?",
  "emoji": "😴",
  "color": "#4C1D95",
  "description": "One-minute skill: Why Do We Dream?.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You fly through clouds, talk to a dead relative, show up to work naked. Why does your brain do this?",
      "visual": "☁️"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Theories abound: dreams process emotions, consolidate memories, rehearse threats, or are just neural noise.",
      "visual": "🤔"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Modern evidence supports multiple functions—dreams likely serve emotion regulation and creative problem-solving.",
      "visual": "🎨"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "REM dreams strip the emotional charge from difficult memories, acting as overnight therapy.",
      "visual": "🩹"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "People deprived of REM sleep become increasingly anxious and emotionally volatile—dreams keep you stable.",
      "visual": "😰"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Dreams aren't random nonsense.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What emotional function do REM dreams appear to serve?",
    "options": [
      "Stripping emotional charge from difficult memories",
      "Creating new fears",
      "Erasing all emotions permanently"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
